Sure! You can easily place an SVG icon inside or next to an input on the right side and make it clickable. Here's a simple example using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8" />
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
<title>Input with Clickable Search Icon</title>
<style>
  .input-container {
    position: relative;
    display: inline-block;
  }

  .input-container input {
    padding-right: 30px; /* Make space for the icon */
    height: 30px;
    font-size: 16px;
  }

  .input-container svg {
    position: absolute;
    top: 50%;
    right: 5px;
    transform: translateY(-50%);
    width: 18px;
    height: 18px;
    fill: #555;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="input-container">
  <input type="text" id="searchInput" placeholder="Type to search..." />
  <!-- Example reading glass (magnifying glass) SVG icon -->
  <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" id="searchIcon" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
    <path d="M15.5 14h-.79l-.28-.27a6.471 6.471 0 001.48-5.34C15.14 6.33 12.81 4 9.92 4S4.7 6.33 4.7 9.39c0 3.07 2.33 5.4 5.22 5.4 1.61 0 3.06-.75 4-1.91l.27.28v.79l4.25 4.25 1.5-1.5-4.24-4.25zM9.92 13c-2 0-3.6-1.6-3.6-3.61s1.6-3.6 3.6-3.6 3.6 1.6 3.6 3.6-1.6 3.61-3.6 3.61z"/>
  </svg>
</div>

<script>
  const input = document.getElementById('searchInput');
  const icon = document.getElementById('searchIcon');

  icon.addEventListener('click', () => {
    const value = input.value.trim();
    if(value) {
      onClick(value);
    } else {
      alert('Please enter something');
    }
  });

  function onClick(value) {
    // Your logic here. For demo, just alert:
    alert('Input value submitted: ' + value);
  }
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

### Explanation:
- The container `.input-container` is set `position: relative` so we can absolutely position the SVG inside it.
- The input padding on the right ensures that the text does not overlap with the SVG icon.
- The SVG icon is positioned `absolute` on the right side, vertically centered.
- The SVG has a `cursor: pointer` so it’s clear you can click it.
- In JavaScript, the SVG has a click event listener that reads the current input value and calls `onClick` if there's input, or alerts to enter something.

If you want the icon inside the input as a button (for accessibility), you can do it with a button element wrapping or other approach as well. Let me know if you want that!

